# Raised 206 -> 207 _scoped methods and 1 new PgPool file (async-promote PR,
# 2026-09-08): Store::link_node_atoms_batch_scoped (store/mod.rs).
#
# WHY THIS COULDN'T AVOID BEING A NEW TWIN. `promote_node_scoped` used to
# copy+link a promoted reef's corals per-atom, INSIDE the HTTP request that
# called it — a production promotion of a 3,129-coral reef performed on the
# order of 10,000+ sequential round trips this way and never completed
# (every prior successful promotion in that tenant DB had carried 1-2
# corals; this shape had simply never been exercised at real scale). The
# fix: `promote_node_scoped` now publishes the node and enqueues a
# `PromoteNode` job; a new worker (`workers::promote`) does the actual
# copy+link+derive tail, batched, per CHUNK_SIZE-sized chunk, each chunk
# inside ONE scoped transaction so the atom copy, the link, and the
# derivation-enqueue commit or roll back together. `link_node_atoms_batch_scoped`
# is the batched sibling of the ALREADY-existing, already-frozen
# `link_node_atom_scoped` (this baseline's 206) — same reason that one is a
# `_scoped` twin in the first place: it must run inside the CALLER's already-
# open transaction (the chunk's), not open its own. Checked the guard's own
# suggested fix ("take the scope from the handle instead of passing it as a
# parameter") first: that presumes a `Db<'a>` handle from `store.begin(scope)`
# to take it from, which does not exist yet (the restructure this guard is
# rate-limiting has not started, per its own header) — there is no non-`_scoped`
# primitive in this codebase today that runs a batched, set-based INSERT
# inside a caller-supplied transaction. Written as a NEW method rather than a
# rewrite of `link_node_atom_scoped` in place because that method has other,
# genuinely-one-atom-at-a-time callers (manual single-coral attach,
# `workers::extract`'s per-atom entity linking) this PR does not touch — see
# both methods' own doc comments in store/mod.rs.
#
# `workers/promote.rs` is a NEW file threading `&PgPool`/`t.pool` to
# `tenancy::scope::begin_account_scoped`/`begin_tenant_scoped` per chunk —
# the same "tenant-harness queue worker taking a bare `PgPool`" shape already
# frozen into this baseline for `workers/slack_name_backfill.rs`,
# and every other queue worker below. Not a
# new pattern this raise introduces.
#
# HAND EDIT, Gmail Add-on pairing (2026-09-08): backend/src/routes/addin.rs
# is a NEW control-plane-only route file — `pair/start`/`pair/approve`/
# `pair/poll`/token revoke for the Gmail Add-on's PAT credential
# (migrations 0210_addin_tokens/0211_addin_pairing_codes, renumbered past
# this same PR's own 0209_job_progress.sql on rebase). Same shape and
# justification as routes/oauth.rs, already frozen above: a bare `&PgPool`
# (via `state.control_db()`) reading/writing control-only tables
# (`addin_tokens`, `addin_pairing_codes`) that have no tenant twin by
# design (see those migrations' own headers) and are never opened through
# `store.begin(scope)` — there is no per-request tenant scope for two of
# these three routes to derive one from (`pair/start`/`pair/poll` run
# unauthenticated, before any `Tenant`/`TenancyDecision` exists at all).
# Routing this through `Store` would mean adding control-table methods to a
# CONTENT-plane trait for a feature the trait's own scope note (`Tenant`'s
# doc comment) says explicitly it does not cover. Not a new pattern this PR
# introduces — the same control-only credential-table shape `oauth.rs`/
# `connectors.rs` already carry, on a new file because this is a new,
# narrower credential type (an add-on PAT, not a third-party OAuth token).
# Does not itself change `scoped_methods` — merged past the async-promote
# raise above, which does.
#
