# `cleanup` helpers that seed and tear down fixture rows directly. Same
# shape and same precedent as `routes/me.rs`/`routes/prefs.rs` above: the
# PRODUCTION handler under test (`create_graph`) holds no PgPool of
# their own — both already receive their content-plane access through the
# `Tenant` extractor's `store: Option<Arc<dyn Store>>` (routed through
# `Store::account_is_real_org`, the new method this PR adds), and the test
# module's `fn tenant(pool: sqlx::PgPool) -> Tenant` only ever WRAPS a raw
# pool into `Tenant.pool` to hand-build an extractor value outside the real
# HTTP request path — the identical `Tenant { store, pool, decision }`
# construction `routes::usage::pg_tests`'s own `personal_tenant` helper
# already uses (that file IS already in this baseline, below, for this exact
# same test-only shape — a fourth instance of a pattern already frozen in
# three times over, not a new one). Not a production conversion site the
# Scope/Db restructure would
# ever touch: a test fixture's pool exists to INSERT rows for the test to
# read back, not to serve a request, and would be rewritten (or deleted)
# alongside its test the day that restructure actually starts. Adding a
# test-only entry rather than aliasing the type in the test — the guard's
# own header names a type alias as an evasion, and `routes/me.rs`'s own
# entry above already rejected that path for the same reason.
#
# NOTED FOR THE GUARD'S OWN "WHAT A GREEN RUN DOES NOT PROVE" LIST: this
# script's `list_pgpool_files` scans a file's raw text for the literal
# substring `PgPool`, ignoring only full-line comments — it has NO notion of
# `#[cfg(test)]` at all, so it cannot itself tell a test-only occurrence
# apart from a production one. That distinction is made by a human, once,
# in a hand-edit comment like this one (and `routes/me.rs`'s/`routes/
# prefs.rs`'s before it) — not by the script. A file could carry this exact
# comment while its production code grows a REAL PgPool later, and the
# script would not notice the comment's premise had gone stale; only a
# reviewer re-reading the diff would.
