# HAND EDIT, prefs role-check PR: `routes/prefs.rs` mentions `PgPool` ONLY in
# its `#[cfg(test)] mod pg_tests` harness (PgPoolOptions/PgConnection to
# create + migrate a throwaway control database), exactly like
# routes/org_invites.rs above. The handler itself holds no PgPool: the org
# check it gained routes through `Store::account_is_org` via
# `state.control_store()`, which is the shape this ratchet is steering toward.
# Adding a test-only entry rather than aliasing the type in the test — the
# guard's own header names a type alias as an evasion.
#
# HAND EDIT, claim-based tenancy §Phase 3 PR 3.2 (PR #806, 2026-09-07):
# `mcp/tenant_selection.rs` is a NEW file (the `mcp_tenant_selection` table's
# reader/writer + the pure `resolve_selected_tenant` decision core it feeds).
# Checked routing it through `Store` first — this is the guard's own
# suggested fix, and `routes/me.rs` above is a real precedent for a
# CONTROL-PLANE-ONLY, unscoped pointer table (`user_active_graph`) doing
# exactly that (`Store::user_active_graph`/`set_user_active_graph` via
# `state.control_store()`). `mcp_tenant_selection` is the SAME shape by
# itself: a simple keyed control-plane row, no RLS, no tenant-plane twin.
#
# It does NOT convert cleanly, for one reason that isn't cosmetic:
# `live_membership_for_selection` (the fresh live-membership re-check this
# module's whole design hinges on) calls `middleware::auth::
# live_org_membership` DIRECTLY — reused on purpose, not reimplemented, per
# this module's own doc ("never a hand-rolled copy" of a security-critical
# query a claim-sourced org id already gets on every request). That function
# takes `&sqlx::PgPool`, not `&dyn Store`, and it is a heavily-relied-upon,
# already-baselined (`middleware/auth.rs` above) piece of the auth spine —
# widening its signature to route through `Store` is a bigger, riskier change
# than this PR's job, for a caller unrelated to why it exists. The
# alternative, adding a Store method that re-queries the same thing, is
# exactly the duplicate-copy risk the reuse was chosen to avoid.
#
# Converting ONLY `load_stored_selection`/`store_selection`/`clear_selection`
# (the three functions that COULD move, mirroring `user_active_graph`
# exactly) was considered and rejected as not worth doing: `PgPool` would
# still appear in this same file via `live_membership_for_selection`'s
# parameter regardless, so a partial conversion adds a new `Store` method
# pair for no reduction in what this guard is measuring — the file stays
# flagged either way. Full entry, not a test-only one: unlike `routes/me.rs`/
# `routes/prefs.rs`, this file's PRODUCTION code (not just its `pg_tests`
# harness) holds the pool, the same shape already baselined for
# `middleware/auth.rs`/`mcp/auth.rs` right above.

# HAND EDIT, personal-orgs PR (2026-09-07): `bin/create_personal_orgs.rs` is a
# NEW operator binary — the mechanism that mints the Clerk organization
# standing for one person's own ocean (migration 0208_personal_orgs.sql) and
# records it. `--update` is not the channel for this and was not used: that
# flag can only ever lower the count and remove paths, by design. This is the
# hand edit the guard's own message asks for.
#
# Checked routing it through `Store` first, which is the guard's suggested
# fix. It does not apply here, for the same reason every other entry under
# `backend/src/bin/` in this baseline gives — `backfill_billing_enforcement.rs`,
# `grant_plan.rs`, `migrate_control.rs`, `migrate_tenants.rs`,
# `export_member_ocean.rs`, `reextract.rs`,
# `migrate_personal_to_member_ocean.rs` and the rest of the backfill_* family:
# an operator-run one-shot owns its own pool, opens it from
# `migrator_database_url()`, and never serves a request. There is no
# `AppState`, no `state.control_store()`, and no request scope for
# `store.begin(scope)` to be derived from. `backend/src/bin` is already the
# sanctioned home for exactly this shape, per those entries and per
# `check_no_cross_plane_promotion.sh`'s SCAN_DIRS (routes/services/mcp)
# deliberately excluding it.
#
# The pool here is about as small as this shape gets: two statements against
# ONE control-plane table with no RLS policy (0204's header says why) — a
# `SELECT clerk_org_id FROM personal_orgs WHERE clerk_user_id = $1` and an
# `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`. Adding a `Store` method pair for them
# would raise `scoped_methods` above instead, trading one frozen metric for
# another without shrinking the restructure's bill — the same reasoning the
# `services/retraction_audit.rs` entry above records. Not a new pattern this
# PR introduces, and not a conversion site the restructure gains: the file has
# no request-path callers.
#
# Spelling the type some other way to slip past the scanner (a `PgConnection`,
# a local alias) was considered and rejected outright: this guard's own header
# names a type alias as an evasion, and a serial one-shot using a single
# connection purely to avoid the word `PgPool` would be exactly that.

# HAND EDIT, personal-orgs step 3 (2026-09-08):
# `bin/adopt_personal_org_account.rs` is a NEW operator binary — it gives a
# personal organization's identity to the account that already holds the
# person's data and deletes the empty account Clerk's `organization.created`
# webhook made. `--update` is not the channel for this and was not used: that
# flag can only lower the count and remove paths. This is the hand edit the
# guard's own message asks for, same as `create_personal_orgs.rs` above.
#
# Routing it through `Store` was considered and rejected on this one
# specifically: the tool's whole job is a single transaction that DELETEs an
# `accounts` row and UPDATEs another while `account_members_account_org_fk` is
# DEFERRED. `SET CONSTRAINTS` is connection-scoped, so the work has to hold one
# connection for the duration — which is exactly the raw-pool shape the
# restructure is removing elsewhere, and exactly what correctness requires
# here. Spelling the type `PgConnection` to slip past the scanner was also
# rejected: the guard's header names a type alias as an evasion.

# HAND EDIT, promote-content-loss fix (PR #838, 2026-09-09): two NEW PgPool
# files, `backend/src/promoted_blob_backfill.rs` (the backfill core) and
# `backend/src/bin/backfill_promoted_blobs.rs` (its thin binary wrapper).
# `--update` is not the channel for this and was not used: that flag can
# only ever lower the count and remove paths. This is the hand edit the
# guard's own message asks for.
#
# Same family, and same reasoning, as every other `backfill_*`/`reextract`/
# `judgement_backfill`/`self_reef_backfill` entry already frozen in this
# baseline (see `backend/src/reextract.rs`/`backend/src/judgement_backfill.rs`
# above): an operator-run one-shot that owns its own pool — opened from
# whatever DSN the operator points it at (control plane or one tenant DB per
# run, per the module's own "Run once per tenant database" doc) — and never
# serves a request. There is no `AppState`, no `state.control_store()`, and
# no request scope for `store.begin(scope)` to be derived from; `backend/src/
# bin` is already the sanctioned home for exactly this shape. Routing it
# through `Store` was checked and rejected for the same structural reason
# `judgement_backfill.rs`/`reextract.rs` give: this tool walks every
# promoted atom across every graph/account in a database by design (finding
# the rows a per-account boundary would hide is the entire point), which a
# scope bound to a single account cannot express. It also carries the same
# preflight-not-trust discipline as those two — `promoted_blob_backfill::
# assert_policy_bypass` — with its own negative control in
# `backend/tests/promoted_blob_backfill_pg.rs` (see that suite and
# `scripts/rls_coverage.manifest`'s matching entry for the detail). Not a new
# pattern this PR introduces — the fourth one-shot of this exact shape now
# frozen into this baseline.
